# Docker Images, Containers & Virtual Machines: A Complete Study Guide

---

## Part 1: How Docker Images Are Built — The Layer System

A Docker image is not a single monolithic file. It is a **stack of read-only layers**, where each layer represents one piece of your application's environment.

Think of it like a cake:

```
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│     Your Application Code   │  ← top layer
├─────────────────────────────┤
│     Anaconda / pip libs     │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│        Python 3.x           │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│     MongoDB / MySQL         │
├─────────────────────────────┤
│   Linux Base Image (tiny)   │  ← bottom layer (base image)
└─────────────────────────────┘
```

Each layer only stores the **diff** — the changes it adds on top of the layer below it. This is why Docker images are compact and why builds are fast (unchanged layers are cached and reused).

### Your Lambda Dockerfile in This Mental Model

Your Dockerfile from the previous discussion maps perfectly onto this:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CMD: main.lambda_handler           │  ← entry point layer
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  ENV variables (REGION, TABLE etc.) │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  COPY main.py + CSV                 │  ← your application code
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  RUN pip install -r requirements    │  ← your dependencies
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│  public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.12  │  ← AWS base image (bottom)
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

