## Part 2: Docker Image vs. Docker Container

This is one of the most commonly confused distinctions in Docker. Here is the clean mental model:

$$\text{Docker Image} \xrightarrow{\texttt{docker run}} \text{Docker Container}$$

| | **Docker Image** | **Docker Container** |
|---|---|---|
| **What it is** | A static, packaged artifact — all layers stacked together | A live, running instance of that image |
| **State** | Inert — sits on disk, does nothing | Active — consumes CPU and memory |
| **Shareable?** | Yes — push to ECR, Docker Hub, etc. | No — runs on a specific host |
| **Analogy** | A blueprint / recipe / `.exe` file | The running process / the built house |
| **OOP equivalent** | A **Class** definition | An **Object** (instance of the class) |

$$\text{Class (Image)} \xrightarrow{\text{instantiate}} \text{Object (Container)}$$

### What Happens When You Run an Image

When you execute `docker run <image>`:

1. Docker reads all the stacked layers of the image
2. It creates an isolated **container environment** on your host machine
3. All dependencies defined in the image are available inside that environment
4. Your application starts **within** that environment
5. When the process exits, the container stops (but the image remains untouched)

One image can spawn **many containers simultaneously** — each isolated from the others.

