## Part 3: Docker vs. Virtual Machines

To understand the difference, you first need to understand how an operating system is structured.

### How an OS Is Structured

Every operating system has two fundamental layers:

```
┌──────────────────────────┐
│     Application Layer    │  ← Layer 2: UI, apps, shell, utilities
├──────────────────────────┤
│      OS Kernel Layer     │  ← Layer 1: communicates with hardware
├──────────────────────────┤
│        Hardware          │  (CPU, RAM, Storage)
└──────────────────────────┘
```

- The **kernel** is the core — it talks directly to hardware. It manages memory, processes, and I/O.
- The **application layer** is everything on top — the UI, shell, installed programs, etc.

This is why different Linux distributions (Ubuntu, Red Hat, Fedora) *feel* different but share the same kernel — their application layers differ, but the underlying kernel is essentially the same.

---

### What Each Technology Virtualizes

Here is the critical architectural difference:

```
┌─────────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────────┐
│         DOCKER              │     │      VIRTUAL MACHINE        │
├─────────────────────────────┤     ├─────────────────────────────┤
│  Container Application      │     │  Guest Application Layer    │
│  Layer (virtualized)        │     ├─────────────────────────────┤
│                             │     │  Guest OS Kernel            │
│  ↕ communicates with ↕      │     │  (its own, fully separate)  │
│                             │     │                             │
│  Host OS Kernel (shared)    │     │  Host OS Kernel             │
└─────────────────────────────┘     └─────────────────────────────┘
```

- **Docker** virtualizes only the **application layer** and talks directly to the *host's* OS kernel.
- **VMs** virtualize **both layers** — they carry their own complete OS kernel, making them a fully self-contained subsystem running inside your host OS.

---

### Detailed Comparison: Docker vs. VM

| Property | Docker Container | Virtual Machine |
|---|---|---|
| **What it virtualizes** | Application layer only | Application layer + OS Kernel |
| **Image / Snapshot size** | Small — typically **megabytes** | Large — typically **gigabytes** |
| **Startup speed** | Very fast — seconds | Slow — minutes |
| **Resource overhead** | Low — shares host kernel | High — needs dedicated RAM, CPU, disk |
| **Isolation level** | Process-level isolation | Full OS-level isolation |
| **Compatibility** | Can have issues (see below) | Runs on any host OS — no issues |
| **Best for** | Microservices, CI/CD, cloud deployments | Running a completely different OS, legacy systems |

---

### The Compatibility Caveat for Docker

Because Docker containers share the host OS kernel, a container must be **compatible with the host's kernel**.

In practice, this mostly matters for **Windows hosts running Linux containers**:

```
Linux Docker Image
        │
        ▼
Needs Linux Kernel to run
        │
        ▼
Windows 10+ → WSL2 provides a Linux kernel → ✅ Works
Windows 7/8 → No Linux kernel support       → ❌ Fails
```

Modern Windows 10 and above ship with **WSL2 (Windows Subsystem for Linux 2)**, which embeds a real Linux kernel — so Linux containers work fine. This compatibility gap is largely a non-issue today, but it's a classic interview question.

VMs have no such problem — since they carry their *own* kernel, a Linux VM runs perfectly on a Windows host and vice versa.

---

## Summary: The Three Key Takeaways

| Concept | One-Line Takeaway |
|---|---|
| **Docker Image** | A portable, layered, static package of your app + all its dependencies |
| **Docker Container** | A live, running environment spawned from an image |
| **Docker vs. VM** | Docker is lighter and faster because it virtualizes only the application layer; VMs are heavier but fully OS-independent |

> **Connecting this to your Lambda work:** When AWS Lambda pulled your image from ECR and ran it, it was spinning up a **container** from your **image** — giving it an isolated application environment backed by AWS's own Linux kernel underneath. You never touched the kernel — Docker handled all of that transparently.